[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Be2 Part 1 / Study 1: White plays 7.O-O / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/d20d584b-986b-4484-ab9e-57b0f95d48fc/studies/76ab1b2a-84ed-4e69-b12f-62fa3da1b5ab/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of our King's Indian Defense course! In Chapter 1, we will delve deep into the main lines of the KID with White pushing all three pawns in the center, developing the kingside quickly, and, in this study, castling on move 7. The first move of our King's Indian Defense is the flexible 1...Nf6. We develop our knight to the f6-square, controlling e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Since White couldn't play e4 because of our knight, they expand in the next best possible way: c4! The two pawns occupy a lot of central space and are trying to suffocate our development. The King's Indian Defense is famous for Black giving up the center early and then trying to win it back with a pawn break in the center. So, we will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White plays the most popular move in this position, which is 3.Nc3. It's played 77% of the time, and was seen in more than 28 million games. The knight now controls the e4-square and White is preparing e4. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter the most often, since it's chosen 55% of the time in this position. It's usually a good idea to grab as much central space as you can in the opening. However, we will start to combat this soon. For now, we will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square, since we don't want to allow White more space than we already have.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {Developing both knights after expanding in the center is the most standard way to continue, and you should face it 30% of the time. White wants to finish kingside development next. In response, we are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves now is Be2, simply activating the bishop and getting ready to castle. In the King's Indian Defense, you should always look for pawn breaks such as ...e5 or ...c5 after having developed the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing it. Their e4-pawn is now weaker, and there are no prospects of pushing e5, so we should make it our primary target with ...Re8.} 8...Re8 9.f3 {Protecting the e4-pawn with f3 is by far the most popular move (played 75% of the time). It does strengthen the center, but it has some drawbacks on its own. Let's play ...c6 for now and potentially prepare ...d5. This also opens up the b6-square for our queen to occupy the diagonal which has just opened up. Black wins 53% of the games in this position, and what's funny is that White makes a mistake over 40% of the time with their next move!} 9...c6 10.Kh1 {White could play Kh1 in this position, which is actually the best move. The king is off the diagonal and prevents any tricks from us. We'll just develop the knight to the standard d7-square in response and await White's next move.} 10...Nbd7 11.Bg5 {The bishop arrives on g5 and pins our knight. We will unpin immediately and improve the queen, attacking the b2-pawn.} 11...Qb6 12.Nb3 {White defends it with Nb3. The queen is also eyeing our d6-pawn now.} 12...a5 {But we can ignore that threat and go for ...a5! White shouldn't capture on d6 because ...a4 is strong, dislodging the knight and leaving the b2-pawn weak again. All in all, we've got a comfortable position here and plenty to hope for.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Be2 Part 1 / Study 1: White plays 7.O-O / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/d20d584b-986b-4484-ab9e-57b0f95d48fc/studies/76ab1b2a-84ed-4e69-b12f-62fa3da1b5ab/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of our King's Indian Defense course! In Chapter 1, we will delve deep into the main lines of the KID with White pushing all three pawns in the center, developing the kingside quickly, and, in this study, castling on move 7. The first move of our King's Indian Defense is the flexible 1...Nf6. We develop our knight to the f6-square, controlling e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Since White couldn't play e4 because of our knight, they expand in the next best possible way: c4! The two pawns occupy a lot of central space and are trying to suffocate our development. The King's Indian Defense is famous for Black giving up the center early and then trying to win it back with a pawn break in the center. So, we will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White plays the most popular move in this position, which is 3.Nc3. It's played 77% of the time, and was seen in more than 28 million games. The knight now controls the e4-square and White is preparing e4. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter the most often, since it's chosen 55% of the time in this position. It's usually a good idea to grab as much central space as you can in the opening. However, we will start to combat this soon. For now, we will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square, since we don't want to allow White more space than we already have.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {Developing both knights after expanding in the center is the most standard way to continue, and you should face it 30% of the time. White wants to finish kingside development next. In response, we are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves now is Be2, simply activating the bishop and getting ready to castle. In the King's Indian Defense, you should always look for pawn breaks such as ...e5 or ...c5 after having developed the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Qxd4 {White decides to capture on d4 with the queen. They want to leave their queen in the center if possible. Now we are going for a move that is played just 18% of the time (against ...Nc6 which is played 70% of the time) and scores very well for Black, which is ...Nbd7. It controls the center but also supports our f6-knight and leaves room for the c-pawn to potentially move.} 8...Nbd7 9.Bg5 {White now pins our knight on f6. It's already sufficiently defended, but we should remove the pin. So we will attack it with ...h6.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {The bishop naturally retreats to h4. Usually pushing pawns in front of your king is dangerous, but the KID is famous for being an extremely solid opening. So we can afford to continue pushing and chasing the bishop away!} 10...g5 11.Bg3 {The bishop goes to g3.} 11...Re8 {We've expanded on the kingside with tempo and have some grasp over the center. It's time to improve the rook and strengthen our central control. Our plans will consist of going after the e4-pawn with ...Nc5 as well. Our f-knight could jump to h5 to attack the bishop on g3.} *

