oped the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing it. Their e4-pawn is now weaker, and there are no prospects of pushing e5, so we should make it our primary target with ...Re8.} 8...Re8 9.f3 {Protecting the e4-pawn with f3 is by far the most popular move (played 75% of the time). It does strengthen the center, but it has some drawbacks on its own. Let's play ...c6 for now and potentially prepare ...d5. This also opens up the b6-square for our queen to occupy the diagonal which has just opened up. Black wins 53% of the games in this position, and what's funny is that White makes a mistake over 40% of the time with their next move!} 9...c6 10.Kh1 {White could play Kh1 in this position, which is actually the best move. The king is off the diagonal and prevents any tricks from us. We'll just develop the knight to the standard d7-square in response and await White's next move.} 10...Nbd7 11.Be3 {White goes for Be3 now, occupying the diagonal.} 11...a6 {We'll slowly prepare ...d5 on the next move. We could lose a pawn there, but then we've got ...Nb6 to get it back immediately.} *

