oped the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing it. Their e4-pawn is now weaker, and there are no prospects of pushing e5, so we should make it our primary target with ...Re8.} 8...Re8 9.f3 {Protecting the e4-pawn with f3 is by far the most popular move (played 75% of the time). It does strengthen the center, but it has some drawbacks on its own. Let's play ...c6 for now and potentially prepare ...d5. This also opens up the b6-square for our queen to occupy the diagonal which has just opened up. Black wins 53% of the games in this position, and what's funny is that White makes a mistake over 40% of the time with their next move!} 9...c6 10.Be3 {The mistake White often makes is Be3. The bishop occupies the diagonal and defends against ...Qb6, but White forgets our other, main idea. We push in the center! The position will open up soon, and hopefully in our favor.} 10...d5 11.cxd5 {White captures on d5 with the c-pawn. Recapturing with the pawn is fine, but I like taking with the knight here. It's tricky since White's pieces are vulnerable on the e-file.} 11...Nxd5 12.Nxd5 {White trades the knights. It wasn't really smart for them to take with the e-pawn. We will simply recapture, of course.} 12...cxd5 13.Bd3 {Defending the position like this is pretty solid, although we get a slight advantage.} 13...Nd7 {Our position is very comfortable. A nice idea from here would be to transfer the knight to f6, but it can also go to e5 or c5.} *

