oped the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing it. Their e4-pawn is now weaker, and there are no prospects of pushing e5, so we should make it our primary target with ...Re8.} 8...Re8 9.f3 {Protecting the e4-pawn with f3 is by far the most popular move (played 75% of the time). It does strengthen the center, but it has some drawbacks on its own. Let's play ...c6 for now and potentially prepare ...d5. This also opens up the b6-square for our queen to occupy the diagonal which has just opened up. Black wins 53% of the games in this position, and what's funny is that White makes a mistake over 40% of the time with their next move!} 9...c6 10.Be3 {The mistake White often makes is Be3. The bishop occupies the diagonal and defends against ...Qb6, but White forgets our other, main idea. We push in the center! The position will open up soon, and hopefully in our favor.} 10...d5 11.cxd5 {White captures on d5 with the c-pawn. Recapturing with the pawn is fine, but I like taking with the knight here. It's tricky since White's pieces are vulnerable on the e-file.} 11...Nxd5 12.Nxd5 {White trades the knights. It wasn't really smart for them to take with the e-pawn. We will simply recapture, of course.} 12...cxd5 13.Nb5 {Trying to move the knight away so that the queen could potentially capture on d5 doesn't work. First, we will just attack the knight.} 13...a6 14.Nc3 {White had to go back to d4. Jumping to c3 loses.} 14...d4 {Now we can play ...d4 with a fork! We are going to win a piece and soon the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Be2 Part 1 / Study 1: White plays 7.O-O / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/d20d584b-986b-4484-ab9e-57b0f95d48fc/studies/76ab1b2a-84ed-4e69-b12f-62fa3da1b5ab/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of our King's Indian Defense course! In Chapter 1, we will delve deep into the main lines of the KID with White pushing all three pawns in the center, developing the kingside quickly, and, in this study, castling on move 7. The first move of our King's Indian Defense is the flexible 1...Nf6. We develop our knight to the f6-square, controlling e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Since White couldn't play e4 because of our knight, they expand in the next best possible way: c4! The two pawns occupy a lot of central space and are trying to suffocate our development. The King's Indian Defense is famous for Black giving up the center early and then trying to win it back with a pawn break in the center. So, we will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White plays the most popular move in this position, which is 3.Nc3. It's played 77% of the time, and was seen in more than 28 million games. The knight now controls the e4-square and White is preparing e4. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter the most often, since it's chosen 55% of the time in this position. It's usually a good idea to grab as much central space as you can in the opening. However, we will start to combat this soon. For now, we will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square, since we don't want to allow White more space than we already have.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {Developing both knights after expanding in the center is the most standard way to continue, and you should face it 30% of the time. White wants to finish kingside development next. In response, we are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves now is Be2, simply activating the bishop and getting ready to castle. In the King's Indian Defense, you should always look for pawn breaks such as ...e5 or ...c5 after having developed the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing it. Their e4-pawn is now weaker, and there are no prospects of pushing e5, so we should make it our primary target with ...Re8.} 8...Re8 9.f3 {Protecting the e4-pawn with f3 is by far the most popular move (played 75% of the time). It does strengthen the center, but it has some drawbacks on its own. Let's play ...c6 for now and potentially prepare ...d5. This also opens up the b6-square for our queen to occupy the diagonal which has just opened up. Black wins 53% of the games in this position, and what's funny is that White makes a mistake over 40% of the time with their next move!} 9...c6 10.Be3 {The mistake White often makes is Be3. The bishop occupies the diagonal and defends against ...Qb6, but White forgets our other, main idea. We push in the center! The position will open up soon, and hopefully in our favor.} 10...d5 11.cxd5 {White captures on d5 with the c-pawn. Recapturing with the pawn is fine, but I like taking with the knight here. It's tricky since White's pieces are vulnerable on the e-file.} 11...Nxd5 12.Bf2 {Declining this sacrifice by moving the bishop away simply leads to an inferior position for White. Instead of trading the knights, we can improve to f4 and put some pressure on the light-squared bishop.} 12...Nf4 13.Bc4 {Placing the bishop on c4 might seem logical, but it's a losing blunder.} 13...Qg5 {Now our queen is threatening checkmate on g5, which will prove to be devastating in the long run. White should have captured our knight on move 12!} *

