oped the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing it. Their e4-pawn is now weaker, and there are no prospects of pushing e5, so we should make it our primary target with ...Re8.} 8...Re8 9.Bf3 {White supports e4.} 9...Nbd7 {But after ...Nbd7 we get a lot of activity and ideas such as ...Ne5 or ...Nc5. If White tries something like Bg5 here, we can chase away the bishop with ...h6 and ...g5. Even ...g4!? works after that.} *

