1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of our King's Indian Defense course! In Chapter 1, we will delve deep into the main lines of the KID with White pushing all three pawns in the center, developing the kingside quickly, and, in this study, castling on move 7. The first move of our King's Indian Defense is the flexible 1...Nf6. We develop our knight to the f6-square, controlling e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Since White couldn't play e4 because of our knight, they expand in the next best possible way: c4! The two pawns occupy a lot of central space and are trying to suffocate our development. The King's Indian Defense is famous for Black giving up the center early and then trying to win it back with a pawn break in the center. So, we will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White plays the most popular move in this position, which is 3.Nc3. It's played 77% of the time, and was seen in more than 28 million games. The knight now controls the e4-square and White is preparing e4. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter the most often, since it's chosen 55% of the time in this position. It's usually a good idea to grab as much central space as you can in the opening. However, we will start to combat this soon. For now, we will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square, since we don't want to allow White more space than we already have.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {Developing both knights after expanding in the center is the most standard way to continue, and you should face it 30% of the time. White wants to finish kingside development next. In response, we are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves now is Be2, simply activating the bishop and getting ready to castle. In the King's Indian Defense, you should always look for pawn breaks such as ...e5 or ...c5 after having developed the kingside. We have to contest the center at some point in order to help our pieces develop and prosper, so we will strike with ...e5 now!} 6...e5 7.O-O {Study 1 deals with White castling, which happens 44% of the time. This not only hides the king, but is a waiting move of sorts, since White is giving us the choice to capture on d4. And that's what we will do. Splitting White's central pawns and leaving the e5-square for our pieces if needed is the way to go.} 7...exd4 8.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing it. Their e4-pawn is now weaker, and there are no prospects of pushing e5, so we should make it our primary target with ...Re8.} 8...Re8 9.Bg5 {White tries pinning our knight, but this is an inaccuracy. We can simply chase away the bishop right now. We will attack it with ...h6 first.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {After the bishop retreats to h4, we can continue chasing it with ...g5!} 10...g5 11.Bg3 {Once the bishop lands on g3, White's plans have failed and their e4-pawn is hanging.} 11...Nxe4 {We will capture it with the knight and we are simply up a pawn with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Be2 Part 1 / Study 2: White plays 7.d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/d20d584b-986b-4484-ab9e-57b0f95d48fc/studies/0b396293-4dc0-43e5-a16b-5839eb7715cb/lines"]

1.d4 {White opens with d4. In the second study of the first chapter, we will take a look at an alternative for White on the critical move 7. Instead of castling and allowing us to capture on d4, they can also push their pawn to d5 and claim even more space, declaring war on the board! Let's begin with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center by White. We will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {Developing the queenside knight first. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter most often. We will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White wants to finish kingside development next. We are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves is Be2. We will strike with ...e5 now to fight in the center.} 6...e5 7.d5 {This study analyzes positions after 7.d5, which arise 30% of the time. This move is ambitious as it gives up the c5-square and closes down the center, but tries to suffocate us by controlling c6 and e6. To come up with a suitable plan against this, we will play ...a5 in order to prevent the quick b4 and c5. Our ideas usually involve ...Nbd7 or ...Na6, aiming for the c5-square.} 7...a5 8.O-O {White decides to castle, which is solid but gives us space and time to proceed with our plans. And we will start with the knight on a6, eyeing that c5-square.} 8...Na6 9.a3 {Going for a3 prepares b4 but seems to be one move too slow. Our knight jumps to c5 anyway and attacks the vulnerable e4-pawn. White has to defend it and doesn't have time for b4 now.} 9...Nc5 10.Bg5 {Pinning our knight instead of defending the pawn directly is certainly an option. As per usual, we will attack the bishop immediately.} 10...h6 11.Bxf6 {White is basically forced to capture in this line.} 11...Qxf6 {But now our queen lands on f6. If White plays b4, you can trade a few of the queenside pieces, and then move the queen to prepare ...f5. The ...f5-break is actually pretty common in the KID as the opener for a kingside attack!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Be2 Part 1 / Study 2: White plays 7.d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/d20d584b-986b-4484-ab9e-57b0f95d48fc/studies/0b396293-4dc0-43e5-a16b-5839eb7715cb/lines"]

1.d4 {White opens with d4. In the second study of the first chapter, we will take a look at an alternative for White on the critical move 7. Instead of castling and allowing us to capture on d4, they can also push their pawn to d5 and claim even more space, declaring war on the board! Let's begin with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center by White. We will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {Developing the queenside knight first. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter most often. We will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White wants to finish kingside development next. We are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves is Be2. We will strike with ...e5 now to fight in the center.} 6...e5 7.d5 {This study analyzes positions after 7.d5, which arise 30% of the time. This move is ambitious as it gives up the c5-square and closes down the center, but tries to suffocate us by controlling c6 and e6. To come up with a suitable plan against this, we will play ...a5 in order to prevent the quick b4 and c5. Our ideas usually involve ...Nbd7 or ...Na6, aiming for the c5-square.} 7...a5 8.O-O {White decides to castle, which is solid but gives us space and time to proceed with our plans. And we will start with the knight on a6, eyeing that c5-square.} 8...Na6 9.a3 {Going for a3 prepares b4 but seems to be one move too slow. Our knight jumps to c5 anyway and attacks the vulnerable e4-pawn. White has to defend it and doesn't have time for b4 now.} 9...Nc5 10.Qc2 {The queen improves to c2 and defends the pawn. Logical, but...} 10...a4 {We have ...a4 now, since the a-pawn is defended twice! White won't be pushing b4 any time soon. Our knight on c5 even has opportunities of transfering to the d4-square via b3 now. The bishop will be activated on d7, and we can also keep in mind some kingside ideas with ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Be2 Part 1 / Study 2: White plays 7.d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/d20d584b-986b-4484-ab9e-57b0f95d48fc/studies/0b396293-4dc0-43e5-a16b-5839eb7715cb/lines"]

1.d4 {White opens with d4. In the second study of the first chapter, we will take a look at an alternative for White on the critical move 7. Instead of castling and allowing us to capture on d4, they can also push their pawn to d5 and claim even more space, declaring war on the board! Let's begin with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center by White. We will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {Developing the queenside knight first. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter most often. We will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White wants to finish kingside development next. We are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves is Be2. We will strike with ...e5 now to fight in the center.} 6...e5 7.d5 {This study analyzes positions after 7.d5, which arise 30% of the time. This move is ambitious as it gives up the c5-square and closes down the center, but tries to suffocate us by controlling c6 and e6. To come up with a suitable plan against this, we will play ...a5 in order to prevent the quick b4 and c5. Our ideas usually involve ...Nbd7 or ...Na6, aiming for the c5-square.} 7...a5 8.O-O {White decides to castle, which is solid but gives us space and time to proceed with our plans. And we will start with the knight on a6, eyeing that c5-square.} 8...Na6 9.Be3 {It's logical for White to try and stop ...Nc5 by placing the bishop on e3. That's why it makes sense for us to attack that bishop!} 9...Ng4 10.Bg5 {You can expect White to attack your queen once your knight moves away from f6. There's nothing to worry about as ...f6 is fine since it comes with tempo. Even though we block our dark-squared bishop, we don't need it for now.} 10...f6 11.Bh4 {White retreats, and here's a move to remember.} 11...h5 {It's important to play ...h5 before you retreat with ...Nh6 and ...Nf7. It helps our kingside attack which could come later, and restricts White. Apart from that, our queenside knight will be coming to c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Main Line Be2 Part 1 / Study 2: White plays 7.d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/d20d584b-986b-4484-ab9e-57b0f95d48fc/studies/0b396293-4dc0-43e5-a16b-5839eb7715cb/lines"]

1.d4 {White opens with d4. In the second study of the first chapter, we will take a look at an alternative for White on the critical move 7. Instead of castling and allowing us to capture on d4, they can also push their pawn to d5 and claim even more space, declaring war on the board! Let's begin with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center by White. We will play ...g6 since we want to fianchetto our bishop on g7.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {Developing the queenside knight first. We'll respond naturally with the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {Three pawns in the center is what you will encounter most often. We will play ...d6 and take control of the e5-square.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White wants to finish kingside development next. We are already able to castle, so that's exactly what we will do.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {One of the most natural moves is Be2. We will strike with ...e5 now to fight in the center.} 6...e5 7.d5 {This study analyzes positions after 7.d5, which arise 30% of the time. This move is ambitious as it gives up the c5-square and closes down the center, but tries to suffocate us by controlling c6 and e6. To come up with a suitable plan against this, we will play ...a5 in order to prevent the quick b4 and c5. Our ideas usually involve ...Nbd7 or ...Na6, aiming for the c5-square.} 7...a5 8.Bg5 {Going for 8.Bg5 is pretty harmless, but it's important to play ...h6 quickly when it happens. We don't want to give White any more time.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {The bishop retreats to h4 now.} 9...Na6 {And it's time to bring out our queenside knight. We have plenty of ideas, involving ...Nc5 as mentioned, as well as ...Bd7 and ...Qe8 to unpin and connect on the light squares. Our f6-knight can then either improve forward or jump backwards to h7, trying to find a better spot. Sometimes, even ...g5!? is an idea.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Be2 Part 2 / Study 1: White Plays 7.dxe5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/3ce6e481-0445-4efa-886b-ed678c4ba071/lines"]

1.d4 {In this first study of Chapter 2, we’re looking at 7.dxe5, the third most played option at amateur level after our KID setup and 6...e5-pawn break. White captures our pawn, opening the d-file in the process. This move appears in 16% of games. Let's reach that position by playing our first move, 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White pushes the c-pawn, gaining more space and central control. 2...g6. We aim to fianchetto our bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White brings out the queen’s knight, developing naturally and controlling the center. We continue with our plan, fianchettoing our bishop. We don’t mind if White occupies the entire center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White occupies more central space with e4, and builds a broad pawn center. We play ...d6 to discourage White from pushing their central pawns further and to prepare a central strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. We safely castle kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {As in the previous chapter, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. We are ready to strike in the center with ...e5. It may look like we're simply losing a pawn, but we'll see that we aren’t.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White immediately captures on e5 with their pawn - this is the subject of study in this chapter. About 15% of games go like this. We recapture. 8.Nxe5 is not popular because Black can recover the pawn with a better position.} 7...dxe5 8.Qxd8 {At the amateur level, most players go for the queen trade in this position, it appears in 72% of games. We recapture with the rook.} 8...Rxd8 9.Bg5 {White develops their bishop, pinning our knight to the rook. This happens 66% of the time. We slide our rook to the e-file, defending the e5-pawn and removing the pin.} 9...Re8 10.O-O-O {27% of players go for opposite-side castling, leading to an interesting and dynamic game. Now ...Na6. Our idea is to get the knight to the c5-square. Our plans also include ...c6, preventing any piece from reaching the d5-square, and kicking the bishop back with ...h6.} 10...Na6 11.Nxe5 {White captures our e5-pawn with their knight. This isn't a free piece: White has Rd8+ and Bxf6 tactics to recover the piece if we capture the knight. We improve our knight, placing it on a more active square and threatening to capture on e4.} 11...Nc5 12.f3 {White defends e4 with their f-pawn. But we can take it anyway with our f-knight. It’s a discovered attack on their knight!} 12...Nfxe4 13.Nxe4 {White recaptures with their knight. We continue trading.} 13...Nxe4 14.fxe4 {White recaptures with the f-pawn, reaching a position with an isolated pawn.} 14...Bxe5 {We recapture with the bishop. We're better in this position thanks to White’s isolated pawn, which we can try to pressure. Our plan could involve advancing our queenside pawns, ...Be6 and ...Kg7 to consolidate the position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Be2 Part 2 / Study 1: White Plays 7.dxe5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/3ce6e481-0445-4efa-886b-ed678c4ba071/lines"]

1.d4 {In this first study of Chapter 2, we’re looking at 7.dxe5, the third most played option at amateur level after our KID setup and 6...e5-pawn break. White captures our pawn, opening the d-file in the process. This move appears in 16% of games. Let's reach that position by playing our first move, 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White pushes the c-pawn, gaining more space and central control. 2...g6. We aim to fianchetto our bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White brings out the queen’s knight, developing naturally and controlling the center. We continue with our plan, fianchettoing our bishop. We don’t mind if White occupies the entire center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White occupies more central space with e4, and builds a broad pawn center. We play ...d6 to discourage White from pushing their central pawns further and to prepare a central strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. We safely castle kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {As in the previous chapter, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. We are ready to strike in the center with ...e5. It may look like we're simply losing a pawn, but we'll see that we aren’t.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White immediately captures on e5 with their pawn - this is the subject of study in this chapter. About 15% of games go like this. We recapture. 8.Nxe5 is not popular because Black can recover the pawn with a better position.} 7...dxe5 8.O-O {White can also delay the queen trade and simply castle. This occurs 12% of the time. We can simply develop our knight to c6, defending the e5 pawn. We have the idea of developing our bishop to the g4-square.} 8...Nc6 9.Qxd8 {White can trade the queens. We recapture with the rook.} 9...Rxd8 10.Bg5 {Black develops the bishop, and pins our knight to the rook.} 10...Re8 {We have the same remedy as in other variations - we simply slide the rook to the e-file. Our plans in this position can include kicking the bishop out with ...h6–g5, and placing our knight on the d4 square. Our light-squared bishop will go to g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Be2 Part 2 / Study 1: White Plays 7.dxe5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/3ce6e481-0445-4efa-886b-ed678c4ba071/lines"]

1.d4 {In this first study of Chapter 2, we’re looking at 7.dxe5, the third most played option at amateur level after our KID setup and 6...e5-pawn break. White captures our pawn, opening the d-file in the process. This move appears in 16% of games. Let's reach that position by playing our first move, 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White pushes the c-pawn, gaining more space and central control. 2...g6. We aim to fianchetto our bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White brings out the queen’s knight, developing naturally and controlling the center. We continue with our plan, fianchettoing our bishop. We don’t mind if White occupies the entire center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White occupies more central space with e4, and builds a broad pawn center. We play ...d6 to discourage White from pushing their central pawns further and to prepare a central strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. We safely castle kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {As in the previous chapter, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. We are ready to strike in the center with ...e5. It may look like we're simply losing a pawn, but we'll see that we aren’t.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White immediately captures on e5 with their pawn - this is the subject of study in this chapter. About 15% of games go like this. We recapture. 8.Nxe5 is not popular because Black can recover the pawn with a better position.} 7...dxe5 8.Bg5 {Another option is to develop the dark-squared bishop, pinning the knight to the queen and delaying the queen trade. This move is played only 6% of the time. We develop our knight via d7, defending the e5-pawn and declining the queen trade.} 8...Nbd7 9.O-O {White castles.} 9...c6 {We remove the option for White to place any piece on the d5 square. Our plans include kicking the bishop back with ...h6, playing ...Qe7 to prepare ...Rd8, and placing our knight on c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Be2 Part 2 / Study 1: White Plays 7.dxe5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/3ce6e481-0445-4efa-886b-ed678c4ba071/lines"]

1.d4 {In this first study of Chapter 2, we’re looking at 7.dxe5, the third most played option at amateur level after our KID setup and 6...e5-pawn break. White captures our pawn, opening the d-file in the process. This move appears in 16% of games. Let's reach that position by playing our first move, 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White pushes the c-pawn, gaining more space and central control. 2...g6. We aim to fianchetto our bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White brings out the queen’s knight, developing naturally and controlling the center. We continue with our plan, fianchettoing our bishop. We don’t mind if White occupies the entire center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White occupies more central space with e4, and builds a broad pawn center. We play ...d6 to discourage White from pushing their central pawns further and to prepare a central strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. We safely castle kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {As in the previous chapter, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. We are ready to strike in the center with ...e5. It may look like we're simply losing a pawn, but we'll see that we aren’t.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White immediately captures on e5 with their pawn - this is the subject of study in this chapter. About 15% of games go like this. We recapture. 8.Nxe5 is not popular because Black can recover the pawn with a better position.} 7...dxe5 8.Qxd8 {At the amateur level, most players go for the queen trade in this position, it appears in 72% of games. We recapture with the rook.} 8...Rxd8 9.Bg5 {White develops their bishop, pinning our knight to the rook. This happens 66% of the time. We slide our rook to the e-file, defending the e5-pawn and removing the pin.} 9...Re8 10.Nd5 {White jumps forward with the knight to the d5 square, improving the piece, adding pressure on f6, and threatening the fork on c7. This is the most played move in the position - 41% of the time. The only way to defend against the threats is to capture the knight.} 10...Nxd5 11.cxd5 {White recaptures with the c-pawn. And we immediately undermine it with our c-pawn. The only challenge is Bc4 - if dxc6, we play Nxc6, recapturing while developing.} 11...c6 12.Bc4 {White improves their light-squared bishop, aiming to recapture the pawn with the bishop. We take their central pawn.} 12...cxd5 13.Bxd5 {White recaptures with the bishop.} 13...Nd7 {We develop our knight via d7, looking to occupy the c5 or b6 squares, which would allow the development of our light-squared bishop. We also want to kick out the dark-squared bishop with ...h6.} *

