1.d4 {In this first study of Chapter 2, we’re looking at 7.dxe5, the third most played option at amateur level after our KID setup and 6...e5-pawn break. White captures our pawn, opening the d-file in the process. This move appears in 16% of games. Let's reach that position by playing our first move, 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White pushes the c-pawn, gaining more space and central control. 2...g6. We aim to fianchetto our bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White brings out the queen’s knight, developing naturally and controlling the center. We continue with our plan, fianchettoing our bishop. We don’t mind if White occupies the entire center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White occupies more central space with e4, and builds a broad pawn center. We play ...d6 to discourage White from pushing their central pawns further and to prepare a central strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. We safely castle kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {As in the previous chapter, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. We are ready to strike in the center with ...e5. It may look like we're simply losing a pawn, but we'll see that we aren’t.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White immediately captures on e5 with their pawn - this is the subject of study in this chapter. About 15% of games go like this. We recapture. 8.Nxe5 is not popular because Black can recover the pawn with a better position.} 7...dxe5 8.Qxd8 {At the amateur level, most players go for the queen trade in this position, it appears in 72% of games. We recapture with the rook.} 8...Rxd8 9.Nxe5 {White may also capture on e5, which looks like a free pawn - but we’ll recover it immediately, reaching an equal position. This capture is played in 18% of games. Take on e4! We have a discovered attack on the e5 knight that lets us win back the pawn straight away.} 9...Nxe4 10.Nxe4 {59% of players will take our knight. And we simply recover the piece by taking the knight with the bishop.} 10...Bxe5 11.Bg5 {White develops their bishop, leaving the b2-pawn undefended but attacking our rook. Danger levels.} 11...Re8 {We have the same remedy as in other variations where White puts pressure on our rook - we slide it to the e-file. If White plays Bf6, we have to be careful not to blunder a fork on f6. Our plan could be ...Nc6, ...Be6 or ...Bf5, and bringing the rooks to the e- and d-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Be2 Part 2 / Study 1: White Plays 7.dxe5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/3ce6e481-0445-4efa-886b-ed678c4ba071/lines"]

1.d4 {In this first study of Chapter 2, we’re looking at 7.dxe5, the third most played option at amateur level after our KID setup and 6...e5-pawn break. White captures our pawn, opening the d-file in the process. This move appears in 16% of games. Let's reach that position by playing our first move, 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White pushes the c-pawn, gaining more space and central control. 2...g6. We aim to fianchetto our bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White brings out the queen’s knight, developing naturally and controlling the center. We continue with our plan, fianchettoing our bishop. We don’t mind if White occupies the entire center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White occupies more central space with e4, and builds a broad pawn center. We play ...d6 to discourage White from pushing their central pawns further and to prepare a central strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. We safely castle kingside.} 5...O-O 6.Be2 {As in the previous chapter, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. We are ready to strike in the center with ...e5. It may look like we're simply losing a pawn, but we'll see that we aren’t.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White immediately captures on e5 with their pawn - this is the subject of study in this chapter. About 15% of games go like this. We recapture. 8.Nxe5 is not popular because Black can recover the pawn with a better position.} 7...dxe5 8.Qxd8 {At the amateur level, most players go for the queen trade in this position, it appears in 72% of games. We recapture with the rook.} 8...Rxd8 9.Nxe5 {White may also capture on e5, which looks like a free pawn - but we’ll recover it immediately, reaching an equal position. This capture is played in 18% of games. Take on e4! We have a discovered attack on the e5 knight that lets us win back the pawn straight away.} 9...Nxe4 10.Nxf7 {White might think they’re winning by capturing on f7 - but it actually blunders a piece! It only works if we take the knight on f7 first, which is not our plan. Still, it’s surprisingly common - played in 37% of games. Our winning move is to take their c3 knight with check!} 10...Bxc3+ 11.bxc3 {White recaptures the piece with the b-pawn. Now White also has doubled, isolated c-pawns.} 11...Kxf7 {We are simply up a piece. Our plan could be to target the c-pawns with ...Be6 and ...Nc6–a5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Main Line Be2 Part 2 / Study 2: Other 6th Moves / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/fa593d78-67ac-485d-ad73-9eef03d49fe5/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we’re going to explore the different options White can play on move 6 other than 6.Be2. Let’s go!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances the c-pawn, grabbing more central space and control. Our plan is to fianchetto the bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight, increasing central influence. We stick to our plan and fianchetto the bishop. We’re fine with White grabbing the center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White expands further in the center with e4, establishing a strong pawn duo. We play ...d6 to limit White’s central expansion and prepare ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White brings out their other knight. We castle short and get our king to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg5 {White can also develop their dark-squared bishop to g5. However, it’s not pinning anything yet, since the e-pawn hasn’t moved. This move is also played 9% of the time. We attack the bishop, forcing it to decide whether to trade or retreat.} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {Another option is to give up the bishop for the knight - played in 17% of cases. We recapture with our bishop - we don’t want to damage our structure.} 7...Bxf6 8.e5 {White advances in the center and attacks our bishop. We take with the pawn.} 8...dxe5 9.dxe5 {White recaptures, hitting our bishop. We slide our bishop back.} 9...Bg7 10.Qxd8 {White decides to trade queens. We recapture with the rook.} 10...Rxd8 11.Nd5 {White places their knight on d5, threatening to win our rook with Nxc7. We develop our knight - leaving c7 undefended, but creating space for our rook and putting pressure on White’s e5-pawn.} 11...Nc6 12.Nxc7 {White captures on c7, attacking our rook. We slide our rook out of danger.} 12...Rb8 13.Nd5 {White’s knight returns to d5.} 13...Bg4 {We develop our light-squared bishop, connecting the rooks. Our plan is to capture on f3 and e5 - and White’s position collapses surprisingly quickly.} *

