[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/fa593d78-67ac-485d-ad73-9eef03d49fe5/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we’re going to explore the different options White can play on move 6 other than 6.Be2. Let’s go!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances the c-pawn, grabbing more central space and control. Our plan is to fianchetto the bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight, increasing central influence. We stick to our plan and fianchetto the bishop. We’re fine with White grabbing the center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White expands further in the center with e4, establishing a strong pawn duo. We play ...d6 to limit White’s central expansion and prepare ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White brings out their other knight. We castle short and get our king to safety.} 5...O-O 6.h3 {White prevents any piece from landing on the g4-square - the best move according to the engine - but it’s only seen in 9% of games. Our response doesn’t change - we immediately strike the center with ...e5.} 6...e5 7.d5 {White locks the center with d5. This weakens the dark squares - d4 and c5 might become useful outposts later on. As in the main line, we want to control the b4-square to place our knight on c5.} 7...a5 8.g4 {White counters by advancing on the kingside. This is the only real idea behind h3, and it’s extremely rare - you’re probably rated above 2000 if you’re facing this. Let's develop our knight to reach c5.} 8...Na6 9.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop, which now controls c5. We don’t mind and still place our knight on the desired square.} 9...Nc5 10.Nd2 {White retreats their knight, likely planning to advance the f-pawn.} 10...c6 {We continue by undermining d5. Our plans include ...a4 to control the knight jump to b3, and ...Bd7.} *

