[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/e0e7dc1d-f8f2-4b39-8f55-965ed4b805a2/studies/fa593d78-67ac-485d-ad73-9eef03d49fe5/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we’re going to explore the different options White can play on move 6 other than 6.Be2. Let’s go!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances the c-pawn, grabbing more central space and control. Our plan is to fianchetto the bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight, increasing central influence. We stick to our plan and fianchetto the bishop. We’re fine with White grabbing the center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White expands further in the center with e4, establishing a strong pawn duo. We play ...d6 to limit White’s central expansion and prepare ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White brings out their other knight. We castle short and get our king to safety.} 5...O-O 6.e5 {Pushing the e-pawn in the center is rare, seen in just 4% of games. It gives us a better and more comfortable position. We trade central pawns.} 6...dxe5 7.dxe5 {White recaptures with the e-pawn, attacking our knight. We first trade queens, forcing White to give up castling rights or recapture with the knight, leaving it placed on the first rank.} 7...Qxd1+ 8.Nxd1 {White recaptures with the knight. We advance the knight that was under attack and threaten the e5-pawn.} 8...Ng4 9.Bf4 {White defends the pawn with their dark-squared bishop.} 9...Nc6 {We continue developing while increasing pressure on e5 - White has no way to hold it. Our bishop will likely go to e6, and we’ll bring the rooks to the center. You’ll mostly face this line at lower levels, but it’s still worth knowing.} *

