1.d4 {In this second study, we’re going to explore the different options White can play on move 6 other than 6.Be2. Let’s go!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances the c-pawn, grabbing more central space and control. Our plan is to fianchetto the bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight, increasing central influence. We stick to our plan and fianchetto the bishop. We’re fine with White grabbing the center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White expands further in the center with e4, establishing a strong pawn duo. We play ...d6 to limit White’s central expansion and prepare ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White brings out their other knight. We castle short and get our king to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bg5 {White can also develop their dark-squared bishop to g5. However, it’s not pinning anything yet, since the e-pawn hasn’t moved. This move is also played 9% of the time. We attack the bishop, forcing it to decide whether to trade or retreat.} 6...h6 7.Bh4 {The most common move - seen in 51% of games - is to retreat the bishop to h4 instead of trading it for the knight. We continue chasing the bishop with our kingside pawns.} 7...g5 8.Bg3 {The bishop keeps retreating. Let’s attack the bishop! After that, we’ll push ...c5 to open up our dark-squared bishop.} 8...Nh5 9.Be2 {White develops their light-squared bishop and is now ready to castle short. We eliminate White’s bishop pair.} 9...Nxg3 10.hxg3 {White recaptures.} 10...c5 {And now ...c5 hits the board, aiming to activate our dark-squared bishop. If White allows it, our knight will head to c6 and our bishop will likely belong on g4.} *

