1.d4 {In this second study, we’re going to explore the different options White can play on move 6 other than 6.Be2. Let’s go!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances the c-pawn, grabbing more central space and control. Our plan is to fianchetto the bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight, increasing central influence. We stick to our plan and fianchetto the bishop. We’re fine with White grabbing the center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White expands further in the center with e4, establishing a strong pawn duo. We play ...d6 to limit White’s central expansion and prepare ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White brings out their other knight. We castle short and get our king to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {The second most popular move is to develop the light-squared bishop to d3, supporting the e4-pawn. This move is played nearly 22% of the time. Even with the bishop on d3, we go for the same approach - striking in the center with ...e5.} 6...e5 7.d5 {White chooses to close the center by pushing the d-pawn forward in 59% of games.} 7...a5 {We play a5 to control the b4 square, since we want to place our knight on c5. Our light-squared bishop will go to d7.} *

