1.d4 {In this second study, we’re going to explore the different options White can play on move 6 other than 6.Be2. Let’s go!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances the c-pawn, grabbing more central space and control. Our plan is to fianchetto the bishop and place it on the long diagonal.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight, increasing central influence. We stick to our plan and fianchetto the bishop. We’re fine with White grabbing the center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White expands further in the center with e4, establishing a strong pawn duo. We play ...d6 to limit White’s central expansion and prepare ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.Nf3 {White brings out their other knight. We castle short and get our king to safety.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {The second most popular move is to develop the light-squared bishop to d3, supporting the e4-pawn. This move is played nearly 22% of the time. Even with the bishop on d3, we go for the same approach - striking in the center with ...e5.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White trades pawns in the center 18% of the time. We simply recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.Nxe5 {White will take our pawn on e5, but we have the same trick as in the last chapter - a discovered attack on the knight. We capture White’s e4-pawn.} 8...Nxe4 9.Nxe4 {White recaptures.} 9...Bxe5 {And we recover the piece. From here, we’ll continue with natural development: the knight goes to c6, the bishop to f5, and our rooks to the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 1: White Plays 7.fxe5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/e59e7659-1239-44de-8d54-b690c99a6a7e/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.e5 {In 18% of games, White plays e5 immediately. We should start trading away White's pawn center.} 6...dxe5 7.dxe5 {A far less common recapture, played only 7% of the time, but worth our attention. We start by trading queens.} 7...Qxd1+ 8.Kxd1 {22% of the time, White recaptures with the king, conceding castling rights.} 8...Ne8 {After retreating the knight to safety, Black will continue undermining White's center with moves like ...Nc6 and ...f6. The knight on e8 will have multiple ways to reenter the game depending on how White reacts.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 1: White Plays 7.fxe5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/e59e7659-1239-44de-8d54-b690c99a6a7e/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.e5 {In 18% of games, White plays e5 immediately. We should start trading away White's pawn center.} 6...dxe5 7.dxe5 {A far less common recapture, played only 7% of the time, but worth our attention. We start by trading queens.} 7...Qxd1+ 8.Nxd1 {White usually (78%) recaptures with the knight to preserve the ability to castle. Let's jump with our knight to e4. Among other things, this stops White from returning their knight to c3 immediately, as we would be able to wreck their queenside pawn structure.} 8...Ne4 9.Bd3 {White develops a piece with tempo. We will move the knight with a tempo on White's bishop. c5 is a nice active square for the knight. It is a little loose, but if attacked, we can either support it with a move like Nba6 or retreat it to e6 if necessary.} 9...Nc5 10.Bc2 {White retreats the bishop to safety.} 10...f6 {...f6 breaks open the center. Black's pieces are more active than White's and have better control of the center. We will play ...Nc6 and force White to have to trade on f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 1: White Plays 7.fxe5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/e59e7659-1239-44de-8d54-b690c99a6a7e/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. We will respond with ...e5, a rare move in the database, played only 4% of the time. It seems like this move shouldn't be possible, since the pawn is attacked three times and defended only once. However, this pawn sacrifice opens up aggressive opportunities for Black.} 6...e5 7.fxe5 {The point of ...e5 was to take on either f4 or d4, as Black would open the e-file and take advantage of White's uncastled king. Thus, 70% of players take with the f-pawn, trying to justify the move f4. We will recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.d5 {It is best for White to decline the pawn here, and most players (71%) do. Let's immediately begin to undermine White's pawn center.} 8...c6 9.Be2 {9. Be2 is also pretty common, played 26% of the time. As with when White plays Bd3, we still strike with ...b7-b5 to continue undermining White's pawn center.} 9...b5 10.cxb5 {White accepts the pawn. With the bishop on e2 instead of d3, this is not as bad for White, since we don't have the pawn fork with ...e5-e4. Nonetheless, we continue with our plan of liquidating White's pawn center.} 10...cxd5 11.exd5 {White recaptures. The b5 pawn currently hinders our queenside development, so it is a good idea to trade it off quickly.} 11...a6 12.bxa6 {White usually accepts the pawn.} 12...e4 {Black has temporarily sacrificed two pawns (a6 is easily recovered) but has a nice position. Black will play Qb6 and try to keep White's king in the center. Black's e-pawn is difficult for White to take, because this would open the e-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 1: White Plays 7.fxe5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/e59e7659-1239-44de-8d54-b690c99a6a7e/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. We will respond with ...e5, a rare move in the database, played only 4% of the time. It seems like this move shouldn't be possible, since the pawn is attacked three times and defended only once. However, this pawn sacrifice opens up aggressive opportunities for Black.} 6...e5 7.fxe5 {The point of ...e5 was to take on either f4 or d4, as Black would open the e-file and take advantage of White's uncastled king. Thus, 70% of players take with the f-pawn, trying to justify the move f4. We will recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.d5 {It is best for White to decline the pawn here, and most players (71%) do. Let's immediately begin to undermine White's pawn center.} 8...c6 9.Bd3 {9. Bd3 is the most common move here, played 36% of the time. We play 9...b5: an excellent move, continuing to undermine White's central pawns. This is yet another pawn sacrifice.} 9...b5 10.cxb5 {Most players accept the pawn here (~36% of the time), which is in fact not a great move. Now that we have diverted the c-pawn from defending d5, we take on d5.} 10...cxd5 11.exd5 {A very natural move, but White is losing if they take this pawn! Note that if White had played 11.Nxd5 instead, we would have exchanged knights on d5 and played ...e4 anyway. By forcing open the e-file, White's uncastled king becomes a fatal weakness. Let's play the fork!} 11...e4 12.Nxe4 {White has to address the fork of their two minor pieces. With the e-pawn gone, the e-file is now completely open. We take the knight.} 12...Nxe4 13.Bxe4 {White recaptures the knight.} 13...Re8 {White is completely busted here. The bishop on e4 will be lost (it can be pressured with ...Bf5 or ...f7-f5). White's knight is tied down defending against ...Qh4. Black's dark-squared bishop breathes fire down the long diagonal, paralyzing White's queenside.} *

