1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. We will respond with ...e5, a rare move in the database, played only 4% of the time. It seems like this move shouldn't be possible, since the pawn is attacked three times and defended only once. However, this pawn sacrifice opens up aggressive opportunities for Black.} 6...e5 7.fxe5 {The point of ...e5 was to take on either f4 or d4, as Black would open the e-file and take advantage of White's uncastled king. Thus, 70% of players take with the f-pawn, trying to justify the move f4. We will recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.d5 {It is best for White to decline the pawn here, and most players (71%) do. Let's immediately begin to undermine White's pawn center.} 8...c6 9.Bd3 {9. Bd3 is the most common move here, played 36% of the time. We play 9...b5: an excellent move, continuing to undermine White's central pawns. This is yet another pawn sacrifice.} 9...b5 10.b3 {Played 30% of the time, White reinforces their pawn chain. However, this lets us play the secondary idea of ...b5, which is to kick White's well-placed knight away from the c3 square by pushing the b-pawn.} 10...b4 11.Ne2 {Most players retreat the knight to e2 here. Note that if White plays Na4, we can already trade pawns on d5 and consider Nxd5 sacrifices, once again exploiting the ...e5-e4 push and White's uncastled king.} 11...Qb6 {Black has a paralyzing grip on the dark squares, preventing White from castling. Note that the e5 pawn is tactically defended by discovered attacks from the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 1: White Plays 7.fxe5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/e59e7659-1239-44de-8d54-b690c99a6a7e/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. We will respond with ...e5, a rare move in the database, played only 4% of the time. It seems like this move shouldn't be possible, since the pawn is attacked three times and defended only once. However, this pawn sacrifice opens up aggressive opportunities for Black.} 6...e5 7.fxe5 {The point of ...e5 was to take on either f4 or d4, as Black would open the e-file and take advantage of White's uncastled king. Thus, 70% of players take with the f-pawn, trying to justify the move f4. We will recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.d5 {It is best for White to decline the pawn here, and most players (71%) do. Let's immediately begin to undermine White's pawn center.} 8...c6 9.Bd3 {9. Bd3 is the most common move here, played 36% of the time. We play 9...b5: an excellent move, continuing to undermine White's central pawns. This is yet another pawn sacrifice.} 9...b5 10.O-O {Played 18% of the time here. This is one of White's better options, but also lets us liquidate the center and equalize. We start trading White's big pawn center.} 10...bxc4 11.Bxc4 {White recaptures. We trade off another of White's central pawns.} 11...cxd5 12.Nxd5 {White should recapture with the knight. Bxd5 should be met with ...Qb6+ followed by ...Nc6.} 12...Ba6 {A key move, trading pieces to neutralize White's activity. We are headed towards a roughly equal endgame.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 1: White Plays 7.fxe5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/e59e7659-1239-44de-8d54-b690c99a6a7e/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. We will respond with ...e5, a rare move in the database, played only 4% of the time. It seems like this move shouldn't be possible, since the pawn is attacked three times and defended only once. However, this pawn sacrifice opens up aggressive opportunities for Black.} 6...e5 7.fxe5 {The point of ...e5 was to take on either f4 or d4, as Black would open the e-file and take advantage of White's uncastled king. Thus, 70% of players take with the f-pawn, trying to justify the move f4. We will recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.Nxe5 {A natural-looking move played 24% of the time, but this is almost losing. White faces too many problems down the semi-open e-file. As is thematic in hypermodern openings, we immediately attack White's center.} 8...c5 9.d5 {White usually pushes the pawn to gain space, but now they are actually lost. Let's attack the knight and prepare to break through on the e-file, where White's uncastled king is still sitting.} 9...Re8 10.Bf4 {Moving the knight would drop the e4 pawn, so White tries to defend the knight while developing a piece. We will use our knight to undermine the white knight's defender. Note that we also control the bishop's only retreating square.} 10...Nh5 11.Nd3 {White retreats the knight and simultaneously defends the bishop. It almost looks like everything is defended, but notice that the e4 pawn is only defended by the c3 knight. Let's take the knight to undermine e4!} 11...Bxc3+ 12.bxc3 {White recaptures the piece.} 12...Rxe4+ {Black finally breaks through. We are winning a piece and also have a crushing attack with White's king out in the open and our pieces ready to join the action.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 1: White Plays 7.fxe5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/e59e7659-1239-44de-8d54-b690c99a6a7e/lines"]

1.d4 {Chapter 3 teaches you about the intimidating Four Pawns Attack. White's big center can scare some players away, but our King's Indian ideas work even against this aggression. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. The bishop is fianchettoed according to plan. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. However, it is difficult for lower-rated players to handle, and if we are better-prepared than our opponent, we can gain a quick advantage. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.e5 {In 18% of games, White plays e5 immediately. We should start trading away White's pawn center.} 6...dxe5 7.fxe5 {7.fxe5 is by far the most common recapture, played 93% of the time. The knight retreats to safety, but is actually still very active on d7, since it will support the thematic ...c7-c5 pawn break.} 7...Nfd7 8.Nf3 {White develops a piece.} 8...c5 {White's center is collapsing and Black is doing very well. The engine gives Black a small advantage. ...cxd4 is coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 2: White Plays 7.dxe5 or 7.d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/847f5daf-4209-40c4-8cdd-d081d71d7b12/lines"]

1.d4 {We continue our exploration of the Four Pawns Attack. This time, we see the pawn capture and advance on move 7, and how that changes our game. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. We fianchetto. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. For what to do after 6.e5, see the previous study. We push 6...e5. We know the pawn sacrifice works.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {Compared to 7.fxe5 (covered in the previous study), dxe5 is the less popular capture, played about 11% of the time. Of course, we still recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.Qxd8 {White usually trades queens here. The recapture is forced.} 8...Rxd8 9.Nxe5 {9.Nxe5 is the most common move here, played 65% of the time. Let's ask White's knight where it wants to go. 9...Nfd7 attacks it twice.} 9...Nfd7 10.Nxd7 {White usually trades knights. Before recapturing, we will take on c3 with check. While we give up the bishop pair, we want to weaken White's pawn structure as much as possible in the coming queenless middlegame/endgame.} 10...Bxc3+ 11.bxc3 {White recaptures. We recapture with the knight, which has many prospective squares to go to from d7.} 11...Nxd7 12.Be3 {White develops a piece and denies our knight the c5 square, and so we put our knight on f6 instead, heading towards the center with a tempo on the e4 pawn.} 12...Nf6 13.e5 {White moves the pawn out of danger and counterattacks our knight.} 13...Ne4 {White's extra pawn isn't felt, and in fact the queenside pawns are serious weaknesses. Black can proceed by doubling rooks on the d-file and eventually playing f6 to break open the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 2: White Plays 7.dxe5 or 7.d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/847f5daf-4209-40c4-8cdd-d081d71d7b12/lines"]

1.d4 {We continue our exploration of the Four Pawns Attack. This time, we see the pawn capture and advance on move 7, and how that changes our game. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. We fianchetto. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. For what to do after 6.e5, see the previous study. We push 6...e5. We know the pawn sacrifice works.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {Compared to 7.fxe5 (covered in the previous study), dxe5 is the less popular capture, played about 11% of the time. Of course, we still recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.Qxd8 {White usually trades queens here. The recapture is forced.} 8...Rxd8 9.fxe5 {Instead of Nxe5, this capture is played about 30% of the time.} 9...Nfd7 {Black should regain e5 (playing ...Re8 if necessary) with a good position, since White's center and dark squares will be quite weak. In case White attacks c7, we have ...Na6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4) / Study 2: White Plays 7.dxe5 or 7.d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/af0a0af7-a039-476d-a15b-cf7e1496349a/studies/847f5daf-4209-40c4-8cdd-d081d71d7b12/lines"]

1.d4 {We continue our exploration of the Four Pawns Attack. This time, we see the pawn capture and advance on move 7, and how that changes our game. ...Nf6 develops a piece and temporarily stops White from taking the full center with e2-e4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White takes more central space with c2-c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop, controlling the center with our pieces in a hypermodern style.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, looking to push e2-e4. We fianchetto. We allow White to take the full center.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White sets up a large pawn center. ...d6 discourages White from pushing their center further and also prepares to eventually strike with ...e5 or ...c5.} 4...d6 5.f4 {5.f4 is played about 18% of the time here. White enters the Four Pawns Attack, which is a very aggressive setup for White against the King's Indian. Let's castle.} 5...O-O 6.Nf3 {Nf3 is the main move here, played 70% of the time. For what to do after 6.e5, see the previous study. We push 6...e5. We know the pawn sacrifice works.} 6...e5 7.d5 {About 11% of the time, White plays 7.d5. Note that in this move order, White has not played fxe5 yet. As a result, d5 is not a good move here, since White should not be letting us open the e-file. We start by taking on f4, turning the e-file into a semi-open file.} 7...exf4 8.Bxf4 {White recaptures the pawn. We have an unexpected capture, but the sacrifice of the knight is easily justified. We grab a pawn, open the e-file, and prepare to play ...Re8.} 8...Nxe4 9.Nxe4 {White has nothing better than to take the knight. We pin the knight to the king and prepare to recapture it with interest.} 9...Re8 10.Bd3 {White defends the knight while developing a piece.} 10...f5 {...f5 increases the pressure on the pinned piece, and the knight is lost. Once Black regains the piece, they will be up a pawn with a much better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 7.d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/e083f898-693a-404d-be68-e33bf221e2c4/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we will take a look at Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3. White opens with the queen's pawn, and we bring our knight to f6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. Black adopts the kingside fianchetto setup which typifies the King's Indian Defense.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. Let's fianchetto our bishop.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Black deters any further expansion by the central white pawns, otherwise e5 attacking the knight might have been annoying.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike at the center, this time with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.d5 {At the lower level, this is played 41% of the time. This move avoids immediate central pawn exchanges. Let's challenge the advanced d-pawn!} 7...e6 8.Qd2 {White continues to develop, and in some positions may be able to exchange off Black's powerful fianchettoed bishop with Bh6. We open the e-file, creating a future open line for the f8-rook coming to e8.} 8...exd5 9.cxd5 {This is the better of the two pawn captures available to White, keeping the e-file partly closed. We prepare an expansion on the queenside with a later ...b5.} 9...a6 10.a4 {Inhibiting Black's plan. Let's centralize our rook.} 10...Re8 11.Bd3 {White plans to finish developing. Let's develop and eye the useful e5-outpost.} 11...Nbd7 12.Nge2 {Now White has almost finished developing. We locate the knight centrally, pressuring the d3-bishop.} 12...Ne5 13.O-O {White brings their king into safety. Retreating the d3-bishop would have allowed ...Nc4, when Black would be able to eliminate the better of the two white bishops.} 13...Nxd3 {Black has the bishop pair, the g7-bishop being especially strong, and good piece play. Additionally, Black may be able to expand on the queenside with the plan of ...Rb8 and ...b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 7.d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/e083f898-693a-404d-be68-e33bf221e2c4/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we will take a look at Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3. White opens with the queen's pawn, and we bring our knight to f6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. Black adopts the kingside fianchetto setup which typifies the King's Indian Defense.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. Let's fianchetto our bishop.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Black deters any further expansion by the central white pawns, otherwise e5 attacking the knight might have been annoying.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike at the center, this time with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.d5 {At the lower level, this is played 41% of the time. This move avoids immediate central pawn exchanges. Let's challenge the advanced d-pawn!} 7...e6 8.Qd2 {White continues to develop, and in some positions may be able to exchange off Black's powerful fianchettoed bishop with Bh6. We open the e-file, creating a future open line for the f8-rook coming to e8.} 8...exd5 9.cxd5 {This is the better of the two pawn captures available to White, keeping the e-file partly closed. We prepare an expansion on the queenside with a later ...b5.} 9...a6 10.Bh6 {This is an inaccuracy which allows Black to gain an advantage with a pretty combination. First: a temporary piece sacrifice, hitting the white queen with tempo and uncovering access to the h4 square for the black queen.} 10...Nxe4 11.Nxe4 {White recaptures. We play the check, which is also a fork.} 11...Qh4+ 12.g3 {Blocking the check and attacking the black queen. Let's recover the piece.} 12...Qxh6 13.Qxh6 {White exchanges queens. We recapture.} 13...Bxh6 14.Nxd6 {White has got the pawn back which they lost on e4.} 14...Nd7 {However, Black is very comfortable here, with open lines and good piece play. Structurally, Black's pawns are better than White's, due to the d5-pawn being slightly weak; while after 15. Nxc8 Raxc8 the black rooks are ready for action on the central files, and the h6-bishop can return to g7 where it menaces the long diagonal. All told, Black's minor pieces are more potent than their White counterparts.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 7.d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/e083f898-693a-404d-be68-e33bf221e2c4/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we will take a look at Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3. White opens with the queen's pawn, and we bring our knight to f6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. Black adopts the kingside fianchetto setup which typifies the King's Indian Defense.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. Let's fianchetto our bishop.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Black deters any further expansion by the central white pawns, otherwise e5 attacking the knight might have been annoying.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike at the center, this time with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.d5 {At the lower level, this is played 41% of the time. This move avoids immediate central pawn exchanges. Let's challenge the advanced d-pawn!} 7...e6 8.Bd3 {This alternative developing move gets the light-squared bishop into the game whilst leaving e2 free for the king's knight. We will open the e-file, creating a potential future open line for the f8-rook.} 8...exd5 9.cxd5 {Recapturing: this is a better capture than with the e-pawn, which would leave the e3-bishop exposed to a future ...Re8 from Black. We prepare to expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 9...a6 10.a4 {Attempting to inhibit the ...b5 idea.} 10...Nbd7 {Developing further. Black will play similarly to the 8. Qd2 line. The king's rook can take up an active post on e8, and the e5 square is an excellent future location for the d7-knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 1: White Plays 7.d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/e083f898-693a-404d-be68-e33bf221e2c4/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we will take a look at Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3. White opens with the queen's pawn, and we bring our knight to f6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. Black adopts the kingside fianchetto setup which typifies the King's Indian Defense.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. Let's fianchetto our bishop.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Black deters any further expansion by the central white pawns, otherwise e5 attacking the knight might have been annoying.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike at the center, this time with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.d5 {At the lower level, this is played 41% of the time. This move avoids immediate central pawn exchanges. Let's challenge the advanced d-pawn!} 7...e6 8.Qd2 {White continues to develop, and in some positions may be able to exchange off Black's powerful fianchettoed bishop with Bh6. We open the e-file, creating a future open line for the f8-rook coming to e8.} 8...exd5 9.exd5 {This is a more risky recapture by White. We immediately place the rook on the open e-file, and pressurize the e3-bishop, pinning it to the king.} 9...Re8 10.O-O-O {Castling into relative safety and unpinning the e3-bishop.} 10...a6 {An exciting middlegame is in prospect, where both players will attempt to storm the opposing king's position. The g7-bishop is a very good piece here. We will be aggressive with ...b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 7.Nge2 or 7.dxc5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/658fbe2f-07d2-4099-ab84-6e9d1a74327c/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we continue to examine Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. We prepare the fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. We fianchetto.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Now ...d6 to deter e5.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.Nge2 {This is the main line at master level, but less common than 7.d5 at the lower level (with 28% of games). We develop and pressure d4.} 7...Nc6 8.Qd2 {White continues to develop. We bring the queen to an active square.} 8...Qa5 9.d5 {This is the most common choice by White here, closing the center. We escape the attack from the d-pawn, and place the knight actively.} 9...Ne5 10.Nc1 {Moving the knight serves two purposes. Firstly, the f1-bishop now defends c4, and secondly White has the future option of Nb3 to force the black queen back.} 10...e6 {A tense struggle awaits. If 11.dxe6 then ...Bxe6 in reply, attacking the c4-pawn; whilst 11.f4 would be met by ...Neg4. Otherwise, Black will look to continue with a plan of ...a6, ...Rb8 and ...b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 7.Nge2 or 7.dxc5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/658fbe2f-07d2-4099-ab84-6e9d1a74327c/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we continue to examine Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. We prepare the fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. We fianchetto.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Now ...d6 to deter e5.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.dxc5 {Played in 17% of games. In this line White accepts the sacrificed pawn. Recapturing is natural and best.} 7...dxc5 8.Qxd8 {In the vast majority of cases, White opts for a queen exchange before capturing again on c5. Naturally Black recaptures.} 8...Rxd8 9.Bxc5 {White captures and poses the question as to whether Black can demonstrate sufficient compensation for the pawn. 9...Nc6 now. Black is a pawn down, but with good piece activity and superior development. The white kingside pieces will struggle to find good squares, whilst the black minor pieces have freer scope.} 9...Nc6 10.Rd1 {Challenging the active rook on d8. The rooks are exchanged.} 10...Rxd1+ 11.Kxd1 {White recaptures. We move the knight once more, gaining time by attacking the bishop on c5 and preparing to compromise White's pawn structure.} 11...Nd7 12.Be3 {The bishop retreats. We take on c3 to shatter the white queenside pawn structure.} 12...Bxc3 13.bxc3 {Of course White has to recapture.} 13...Nde5 {The knight has found a good outpost, and the doubled and isolated c-pawns are a serious weakness for White. Black has excellent compensation for the sacrificed pawn. One good idea for Black will be ...Be6 and ...Rc8 going after the weak c-pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 7.Nge2 or 7.dxc5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/658fbe2f-07d2-4099-ab84-6e9d1a74327c/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we continue to examine Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. We prepare the fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. We fianchetto.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Now ...d6 to deter e5.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.dxc5 {Played in 17% of games. In this line White accepts the sacrificed pawn. Recapturing is natural and best.} 7...dxc5 8.Qxd8 {In the vast majority of cases, White opts for a queen exchange before capturing again on c5. Naturally Black recaptures.} 8...Rxd8 9.Bxc5 {White captures and poses the question as to whether Black can demonstrate sufficient compensation for the pawn. 9...Nc6 now. Black is a pawn down, but with good piece activity and superior development. The white kingside pieces will struggle to find good squares, whilst the black minor pieces have freer scope.} 9...Nc6 10.Nd5 {A more aggressive option for White, moving the knight to a dominant central square and attacking the e7-pawn. We will move our knight to attack the bishop on c5 and discover an attack against the pawn on b2.} 10...Nd7 11.Nxe7+ {White captures the e7-pawn with check. Black exchanges knights.} 11...Nxe7 12.Bxe7 {White recaptures, attacking the rook on d8. We ignore the attack on the rook, and capture on b2 with a counterattack against the a1-rook.} 12...Bxb2 13.Bxd8 {Capturing the black rook. This is better than 13.Rb1, when Black could reply with ...Bc3+ and then move the attacked d8-rook.} 13...Bxa1 {Black has good piece play, easier development and a superior pawn structure, all of which compensate adequately for the pawn deficit. A promising plan is ...Ne5, ...Be6 and ...Rc8, developing the queenside pieces actively and targeting the weak c4-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 7.Nge2 or 7.dxc5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/658fbe2f-07d2-4099-ab84-6e9d1a74327c/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we continue to examine Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. We prepare the fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. We fianchetto.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Now ...d6 to deter e5.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.Nge2 {This is the main line at master level, but less common than 7.d5 at the lower level (with 28% of games). We develop and pressure d4.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {Closing the center immediately, and attacking the c6-knight. The knight moves to an active outpost.} 8...Ne5 9.Ng3 {White moves the knight, opening a line for the f1-bishop to defend the c4-pawn. We continue aggressively, aiming to exploit the g3-knight's lack of squares.} 9...h5 10.Be2 {Ensuring the g3-knight has a retreat square available, while maintaining the defensive connection to the pawn on c4.} 10...e6 {Black undermines the center, intending to exchange on d5 followed by preparing expansion on the queenside with ...a6, Bd7 and b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Samisch (5.f3) / Study 2: White Plays 7.Nge2 or 7.dxc5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f631841b-eb0b-4dfb-ae84-707a41d64532/studies/658fbe2f-07d2-4099-ab84-6e9d1a74327c/lines"]

1.d4 {In this lesson, we continue to examine Black's ideas in the King's Indian Defence when White counters with the Samisch variation, which is characterised by an early f3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White addresses the center. This is the most popular second move for White here. We prepare the fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White continues to develop, aiming to control central squares with their queen's knight. We fianchetto.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White has now fully occupied the center, but Black will play dynamically against it and endeavour to show that it is a target. Now ...d6 to deter e5.} 4...d6 5.f3 {Entering the Samisch variation. This is White's third most common choice here, occurring in 14% of games at the non-master level. We castle.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White develops their dark-squared bishop. We strike with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.dxc5 {Played in 17% of games. In this line White accepts the sacrificed pawn. Recapturing is natural and best.} 7...dxc5 8.Bxc5 {Occasionally White will try capturing on c5 without inserting the queen exchange beforehand.} 8...Nc6 {Black continues with the idea of developing rapidly and playing actively. If White now exchanges queens, we have transposed back to the 8.Qxd8 line. If not, Black has ideas like ...Qa5, ...Be6 and the rooks coming to c8 and d8. White's development is somewhat inhibited by the pawn on f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.g3 {The least popular fifth move that we consider is g3. White prepares to fianchetto their light-squared bishop and then castle. ...d6 is a common move in the King's Indian. Nothing changes and we continue to play our setup.} 5...d6 6.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We play the knight to c6 to push ...e5 in the future.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {White gets their king into safety. Now it's time to strike in the center with ...e5.} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {White wants to simplify the game instead. We'll recapture.} 8...dxe5 9.Qxd8 {White trades the queens off. Let's get the rook into the action as well.} 9...Rxd8 10.Bg5 {White develops the bishop to g5. We bring out the bishop ourselves and attack the c4-pawn.} 10...Be6 11.b3 {White protects it.} 11...h6 {And now we pose a question to the bishop. Black has better control over the center and more space for their pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.g3 {The least popular fifth move that we consider is g3. White prepares to fianchetto their light-squared bishop and then castle. ...d6 is a common move in the King's Indian. Nothing changes and we continue to play our setup.} 5...d6 6.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We play the knight to c6 to push ...e5 in the future.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {White gets their king into safety. Now it's time to strike in the center with ...e5.} 7...e5 8.d5 {White wants to keep the center closed. d5 freed up the c5-square, which we'll use as an outpost for our knight.} 8...Nb8 9.e4 {White builds up the center. ...a5 is an important move to prevent White from kicking our knight from c5 in the future.} 9...a5 10.Ne1 {White remaneuvers their knight via e1. Let's bring the knight to c5.} 10...Na6 11.Nd3 {White's idea is to trade off our knight on c5...} 11...Nd7 {...but we can bring the other knight there, and next go to c5 with either knight, supported by b6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.g3 {The least popular fifth move that we consider is g3. White prepares to fianchetto their light-squared bishop and then castle. ...d6 is a common move in the King's Indian. Nothing changes and we continue to play our setup.} 5...d6 6.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We play the knight to c6 to push ...e5 in the future.} 6...Nc6 7.d5 {White advances with d5 early rather than castling. We will put our knight on the edge of the board, since in King's Indian positions, it's often effective there. This time it targets the c4-pawn.} 7...Na5 8.Nd2 {White protects it with the knight. Now we'll challenge White's central structure with ...c6.} 8...c6 9.b4 {White kicks out our knight on a5... ...but we can ignore it and activate our other knight instead! We're also using the fact that b4 has weakened White's dark-squared diagonal.} 9...Ng4 10.Bb2 {White covers their knight on c3...} 10...Qb6 {...but Black has a potent double attack on the b4 and f2-pawns. Black will win a pawn and emerge with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.g3 {The least popular fifth move that we consider is g3. White prepares to fianchetto their light-squared bishop and then castle. ...d6 is a common move in the King's Indian. Nothing changes and we continue to play our setup.} 5...d6 6.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We play the knight to c6 to push ...e5 in the future.} 6...Nc6 7.d5 {White advances with d5 early rather than castling. We will put our knight on the edge of the board, since in King's Indian positions, it's often effective there. This time it targets the c4-pawn.} 7...Na5 8.b3 {If White decides to cover c4 with the b-pawn, we will still go for our queenside expansion. 8...Nxd5 looks tempting, but it does not work here. 9.Nxd5 Bxa1 10.Bd2.} 8...c5 {We'll grab some space with ...c5 with the plan of expanding with ...a6 and ...b5 later.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.g3 {The least popular fifth move that we consider is g3. White prepares to fianchetto their light-squared bishop and then castle. ...d6 is a common move in the King's Indian. Nothing changes and we continue to play our setup.} 5...d6 6.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We play the knight to c6 to push ...e5 in the future.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {White gets their king into safety. Now it's time to strike in the center with ...e5.} 7...e5 8.d5 {White wants to keep the center closed. d5 freed up the c5-square, which we'll use as an outpost for our knight.} 8...Nb8 9.e4 {White builds up the center. ...a5 is an important move to prevent White from kicking our knight from c5 in the future.} 9...a5 10.a3 {White prepares to expand with b4.} 10...Nbd7 {We go this way to avoid any b4 moves by White. If Be3, Ng4!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.e3 {White can also choose to support the center and prepare to castle quickly with e3, played in 11% of the games. We will push our d-pawn to control the e5-square, and to go ...e5 later.} 5...d6 6.Bd3 {White brings out the bishop. Looks like White has e5 covered, but we can play it anyway! This move often poses problems for White if they decide to go after the e5-pawn.} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {While this isn't a crucial mistake, going after the e-pawn is not a good plan for White. We'll simply recapture.} 7...dxe5 8.Nxe5 {White thinks they've won a pawn, but now Black has a lot of activity for it. Let's check out a nasty line where we can win a piece! The queen will slide to e7, attack the knight, and allow for ...Rd8.} 8...Qe7 9.Nf3 {White retreats. Now we pin the bishop to the queen.} 9...Rd8 10.Qc2 {White gets out of the pin. Let's develop the knight to a6. The idea is to jump to b4 and fork the queen and the bishop. We'll want to rotate to c5 later, that's why ...Na6 is better than ...Nc6.} 10...Na6 11.a3 {White prevents it. Let's attack the bishop once again.} 11...Nc5 12.Be2 {They retreat it.} 12...Bf5 {After ...Bf5, White doesn't have any squares for their queen and has to give up a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.Bg5 {The second most popular move played in 23% of the games is Bg5. White doesn't want to block their bishop with e3 and puts some pressure on the f6-knight. Let's chase the bishop away or force it to take.} 5...h6 6.Bh4 {The bishop retreats. We can kick it even further with ...g5.} 6...g5 7.Nxg5 {White could try this unsound piece sacrifice, aiming to open up our king. We can safely grab the piece.} 7...hxg5 8.Bxg5 {White recaptures. ...d6 yet again covers the e5 and c5-squares and opens up our light-squared bishop to be deployed for defense.} 8...d6 9.e4 {White grabs space in the center. Let's challenge White's central pawns.} 9...c5 10.d5 {White sensibly tries to close down the center. We'll retreat and attack the bishop.} 10...Nh7 11.Bh4 {White retreats.} 11...Nd7 {White's attack won't work here since Black stabilizes their position after taking control over the e5-square with ...Nd7. Black's king is safe since there are enough pieces around to defend.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.Bg5 {The second most popular move played in 23% of the games is Bg5. White doesn't want to block their bishop with e3 and puts some pressure on the f6-knight. Let's chase the bishop away or force it to take.} 5...h6 6.Bh4 {The bishop retreats. We can kick it even further with ...g5.} 6...g5 7.Bg3 {The bishop retreats to g3. And we push ...d6, taking control over some central squares and opening up the light-squared bishop.} 7...d6 8.e3 {White prepares to develop their bishop as well. If e4 then ...Nh5, like in Chapter 1. Let's play ...Nh5 even now. We can plan to win the bishop pair and free up the f-pawn so ...f5 can be possible in the future.} 8...Nh5 9.Bd3 {White brings out the bishop. ...e6 covers the d5-square and also reinforces ...f5, while opening up our queen.} 9...e6 10.O-O {White gets their king into safety.} 10...f5 {And Black expands with ...f5 to gain space on the kingside. The plan is to trade off the bishop on g3 and prepare to break with ...e5, gaining central space as well.} *

