1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.Bg5 {The second most popular move played in 23% of the games is Bg5. White doesn't want to block their bishop with e3 and puts some pressure on the f6-knight. Let's chase the bishop away or force it to take.} 5...h6 6.Bh4 {The bishop retreats. We can kick it even further with ...g5.} 6...g5 7.Bg3 {The bishop retreats to g3. And we push ...d6, taking control over some central squares and opening up the light-squared bishop.} 7...d6 8.e3 {White prepares to develop their bishop as well. If e4 then ...Nh5, like in Chapter 1. Let's play ...Nh5 even now. We can plan to win the bishop pair and free up the f-pawn so ...f5 can be possible in the future.} 8...Nh5 9.Bd3 {White brings out the bishop. ...e6 covers the d5-square and also reinforces ...f5, while opening up our queen.} 9...e6 10.Qc2 {White creates a queen-bishop battery. ...f5 is the move here - gaining space on the kingside and blocking the light-squared diagonal.} 10...f5 11.O-O-O {White castles queenside to attack us on the kingside.} 11...Nc6 {Black will finish the queenside development with ...Nc6 and ...Bd7, with ideas of jumping to b4 and breaking with ...e5 at the right time.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.Bf4 {White opts for Bf4 to grip the e5-square. ...d6 is played as usual.} 5...d6 6.e3 {White keeps developing with e3. Now we'll target the bishop pair. ...Nh5 also allows us to gain more space on the kingside.} 6...Nh5 7.Bg5 {White keeps it by playing Bg5. Let's kick it even further.} 7...h6 8.Bh4 {White retreats the bishop.} 8...g5 {But we'll kick it again and put pressure on the center with ...c5 next. Black has some space to work with on the kingside while maintaining stability in the center. We will be happy to trade on g3 after Bg3.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nc3 Without e4 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/9da83db5-d2b8-4cb6-b6db-f4fced56c529/studies/b784f378-690e-480a-95a1-a3b7858a2a44/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter covers less popular moves against our KID that are not 5.e4. White starts with the queen's pawn, and we persist with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs more squares with c4. We prepare to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6.} 2...g6 3.Nc3 {White doesn't reveal their setup immediately and brings the queenside knight out. We'll bring the bishop to g7 and prepare to castle.} 3...Bg7 4.Nf3 {White develops the other knight. We'll get the king to safety.} 4...O-O 5.e3 {White can also choose to support the center and prepare to castle quickly with e3, played in 11% of the games. We will push our d-pawn to control the e5-square, and to go ...e5 later.} 5...d6 6.Bd3 {White brings out the bishop. Looks like White has e5 covered, but we can play it anyway! This move often poses problems for White if they decide to go after the e5-pawn.} 6...e5 7.O-O {White stays solid and castles instead. Now it's time to support the e5-pawn. We also threaten a fork with ...e4.} 7...Re8 8.dxe5 {White prevents it by trading on e5. We'll recapture.} 8...dxe5 9.e4 {White avoids the fork and also doesn't want their dark-squared bishop stuck behind the e-pawn.} 9...Bg4 {Our bishop gets developed to g4 and pins the knight. We could use the pin to our advantage by bringing up the knight to c6 and aim at d4 next. If our knight has not reached d4, we don't mind trading in variations like h3 Bxf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Setups Without Nc3 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f5e7cd27-d906-4b42-ab2c-4c489641a1a1/studies/015962b1-477f-4fc3-9654-b85ba35d5768/lines"]

1.d4 {This study deals with all the lines from move 3 that do not involve Nc3. White goes for 1.d4. ...Nf6 to initiate the King's Indian.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs some central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nf3 {This is the second most popular choice for White, with little over 4 million games played. Let's fianchetto here.} 3...Bg7 4.g3 {White goes for a Catalan setup with a kingside fianchetto as well. We'll get the king into safety.} 4...O-O 5.Bg2 {White prepares to castle. Now it's time to push ...d6 and prepare for a break with ...c5.} 5...d6 6.O-O {White castles. We go ...Nc6 because the knight may be repositioned to a5.} 6...Nc6 7.d5 {White grabs more space in the center. Our knight targets the c4-pawn.} 7...Na5 8.Qc2 {White protects it with the queen... ...which allows us to gain a tempo with ...Bf5.} 8...Bf5 9.Qa4 {White saves their queen, but it is starting to run short on squares. We'll grab some space on the queenside with ...c5.} 9...c5 10.Nc3 {White brings out the knight.} 10...Bd7 {But after ...Bd7, White has to retreat the queen and lose their c4-pawn. If Nb5, ...a6 comes.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Setups Without Nc3 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f5e7cd27-d906-4b42-ab2c-4c489641a1a1/studies/015962b1-477f-4fc3-9654-b85ba35d5768/lines"]

1.d4 {This study deals with all the lines from move 3 that do not involve Nc3. White goes for 1.d4. ...Nf6 to initiate the King's Indian.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs some central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nf3 {This is the second most popular choice for White, with little over 4 million games played. Let's fianchetto here.} 3...Bg7 4.g3 {White goes for a Catalan setup with a kingside fianchetto as well. We'll get the king into safety.} 4...O-O 5.Bg2 {White prepares to castle. Now it's time to push ...d6 and prepare for a break with ...c5.} 5...d6 6.O-O {White castles. We go ...Nc6 because the knight may be repositioned to a5.} 6...Nc6 7.d5 {White grabs more space in the center. Our knight targets the c4-pawn.} 7...Na5 8.Nbd2 {After Nbd2...} 8...c5 {...the usual ...c5 prepares an expansion on the queenside with ...Rb8 and ...b5. We can also undermine the central structure with ...e6.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Setups Without Nc3 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f5e7cd27-d906-4b42-ab2c-4c489641a1a1/studies/015962b1-477f-4fc3-9654-b85ba35d5768/lines"]

1.d4 {This study deals with all the lines from move 3 that do not involve Nc3. White goes for 1.d4. ...Nf6 to initiate the King's Indian.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs some central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.f3 {This is a high level move seen at the master level. White's idea is to avoid the Grunfeld defense, which we had no intentions of playing anyway, so let's just play the way we do against the Samisch. Let's get the bishop fianchettoed.} 3...Bg7 4.e4 {White grabs the center. ...d6 also prevents White from pushing e5 in some cases.} 4...d6 5.Nc3 {White has been only pushing pawns, so they start to bring out their pieces. We castle, and we have achieved our setup.} 5...O-O 6.Be3 {White keeps developing.} 6...c5 {And with ...c5 we pressure White's center. If they go for d5, we'll undermine it further with ...e6. Otherwise, we can trade on d4 and develop with ...Nc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Setups Without Nc3 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f5e7cd27-d906-4b42-ab2c-4c489641a1a1/studies/015962b1-477f-4fc3-9654-b85ba35d5768/lines"]

1.d4 {This study deals with all the lines from move 3 that do not involve Nc3. White goes for 1.d4. ...Nf6 to initiate the King's Indian.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White grabs some central squares. We will fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.h4 {This is a very rare move, played a few times by GMs. White tries to initiate a quick attack on our kingside. When our opponent starts an attack on the kingside, it is a good idea to take some action in the center. Here, we'll play ...c5.} 3...c5 4.d5 {White wants to keep the center closed.} 4...h5 {We stop White's kingside attacking ideas and now can proceed with ideas similar to the Samisch: Bg7, d6, 0-0 and the critical pawn breaks e6 and b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-English, Anti-Reti / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f69f95dd-8b81-4ce7-880e-6cf33d52c013/studies/cc952166-6074-42bc-8a7e-192a31726c9e/lines"]

1.c4 {The King's Indian setup is quite flexible, and can be played against the English Opening (1. c4) as well. In many lines, it transposes into a variation we have already studied. Here, we look at how to play against a typical English opening setup. We start with ...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {White develops a knight towards the center. We prepare our fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.g3 {In many of White's setups in the English Opening, the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed. We fianchetto our bishop, continuing to our King's Indian setup.} 3...Bg7 4.Bg2 {White fianchettoes their bishop. We get our king to safety. We do not need ...d6 yet.} 4...O-O 5.e4 {White plays 5. e4 here about 27% of the time. As is typical in the King's Indian Defense, we play ...d6 to restrict the e-pawn, open up the d7 square, and control e5 and c5.} 5...d6 6.Nge2 {If White plays Nf3, the position will likely transpose into something we have seen before. We recommend the move ...c5 here.} 6...c5 7.O-O {White castles. Now that the c-pawn has advanced, we can put the knight on c6 without worrying about blocking the pawn.} 7...Nc6 8.d3 {Black has very strong control over the d4 square, so White usually settles for playing d3 here (60% of the time). ...a6 is a flexible move, restricting White's knight and preparing to support b7-b5.} 8...a6 9.h3 {White also makes a waiting move that restricts the g4 square. We move our rook to b8. It will prepare b7-b5.} 9...Rb8 10.a4 {White tries to shut down our plan of pushing the b-pawn.} 10...Ne8 {This move opens up the bishop's line of sight and potentially prepares ...Nd4!?. We may also play ...Nc7 to support the ...b7-b5 push.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-English, Anti-Reti / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f69f95dd-8b81-4ce7-880e-6cf33d52c013/studies/cc952166-6074-42bc-8a7e-192a31726c9e/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...Nf6 2.g3 {One possible way White can play is with the King's Indian Attack: that is, playing the King's Indian setup themselves. In this variation, we will explore what we can do in response to this. We start by fianchettoing our bishop as usual.} 2...g6 3.Bg2 {White fianchettoes their bishop, and we will do the same.} 3...Bg7 4.O-O {White castles. This is standard procedure in the Reti. It gives us time to castle as well and achieve our setup.} 4...O-O 5.d3 {By playing d3, White has played exactly the setup that we usually play. One approach here is to play ...d5 instead of ...d6, occupying the center, since White has forgotten about the center. In doing so, we break the symmetry of the position.} 5...d5 6.Nbd2 {White develops a piece to a natural square. We continue taking central space.} 6...c5 7.e4 {White strikes at our central pawns, just as we would when playing e7-e5 in a main line King's Indian Defense. We continue developing our queenside knight to c6. Note that e4-e5 can be met with Ng4, which would attack the pawn three times.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White adds another supporter to the e-pawn and threatens to push it forward. We block the pawn and take the full center.} 8...e5 9.exd5 {White trades a pair of central pawns. We recapture. Once the knight moves, e5 gets another defender in the form of the g7-bishop.} 9...Nxd5 10.Nc4 {10.Nc4 is played over 50% of the time here. White has three attackers on e5, and we have two defenders. ...f6 is an interesting approach, reinforcing the center with a pawn, and simultaneously stopping White from putting a piece on g5.} 10...f6 11.a4 {White expands on the queenside. The light-squared bishop will be good on e6, where it covers some of the light squares that we weakened by playing ...f7-f6.} 11...Be6 12.c3 {c3 prepares to eventually break with d3-d4.} 12...Qd7 {Reinforcing the loose bishop and unpinning the e5 pawn. Black has a very solid position. In some lines we will play ...Rf7 and reroute the bishop to f8, where it will be less caged in. Other common ideas involve playing ...Rad8 and ...b6.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-English, Anti-Reti / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f69f95dd-8b81-4ce7-880e-6cf33d52c013/studies/cc952166-6074-42bc-8a7e-192a31726c9e/lines"]

1.c4 {The King's Indian setup is quite flexible, and can be played against the English Opening (1. c4) as well. In many lines, it transposes into a variation we have already studied. Here, we look at how to play against a typical English opening setup. We start with ...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {White develops a knight towards the center. We prepare our fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.g3 {In many of White's setups in the English Opening, the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed. We fianchetto our bishop, continuing to our King's Indian setup.} 3...Bg7 4.Bg2 {White fianchettoes their bishop. We get our king to safety. We do not need ...d6 yet.} 4...O-O 5.Nf3 {5.Nf3 is played about 27% of the time here. ...d6 is still a good move here, controlling some central squares, opening up the d7 square for our knight or bishop, and potentially looking to play e7-e5 or c7-c5.} 5...d6 6.O-O {White castles. White has decided to delay playing e2-e4 or d2-d4, so now we can start occupying the center ourselves.} 6...e5 7.d3 {7.d3 is played here 48% of the time. Note that 7.d4, played 30% of the time, transposes back into a previously studied line after ...Nc6 (see Chapter 5). Black has many choices, but ...c6 is a nice move, blunting White's light-squared bishop and restricting the c3 knight.} 7...c6 8.Rb1 {Rb1 is also played about 26% of the time. We will play ...a5. White's idea is to expand on the queenside, and so we prepare to meet b2-b4 by capturing the pawn.} 8...a5 9.a3 {White prepares b2-b4. ...h6 is a flexible waiting move, giving the king another square and restricting White's dark-squared bishop and knight.} 9...h6 10.b4 {White plays the move they have been preparing. We take the pawn.} 10...axb4 11.axb4 {White takes back. We develop the light-squared bishop to e6, where it may potentially support d6-d5 in the distant future.} 11...Be6 12.b5 {White often continues pushing their queenside pawns. We finish developing our pieces.} 12...Nbd7 13.bxc6 {White captures the pawn.} 13...bxc6 {Black recaptures, with a rock-solid position. After connecting rooks and finishing development, we will prepare to break in the center with d6-d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Anti-English, Anti-Reti / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/5ce335b2-22e1-434e-a2f4-de67b83d4807/chapters/f69f95dd-8b81-4ce7-880e-6cf33d52c013/studies/cc952166-6074-42bc-8a7e-192a31726c9e/lines"]

1.c4 {The King's Indian setup is quite flexible, and can be played against the English Opening (1. c4) as well. In many lines, it transposes into a variation we have already studied. Here, we look at how to play against a typical English opening setup. We start with ...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.Nc3 {White develops a knight towards the center. We prepare our fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.g3 {In many of White's setups in the English Opening, the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed. We fianchetto our bishop, continuing to our King's Indian setup.} 3...Bg7 4.Bg2 {White fianchettoes their bishop. We get our king to safety. We do not need ...d6 yet.} 4...O-O 5.Nf3 {5.Nf3 is played about 27% of the time here. ...d6 is still a good move here, controlling some central squares, opening up the d7 square for our knight or bishop, and potentially looking to play e7-e5 or c7-c5.} 5...d6 6.O-O {White castles. White has decided to delay playing e2-e4 or d2-d4, so now we can start occupying the center ourselves.} 6...e5 7.d3 {7.d3 is played here 48% of the time. Note that 7.d4, played 30% of the time, transposes back into a previously studied line after ...Nc6 (see Chapter 5). Black has many choices, but ...c6 is a nice move, blunting White's light-squared bishop and restricting the c3 knight.} 7...c6 8.Bg5 {Played about 26% of the time. White develops a piece and pins the Black knight. With 8...h6, we immediately ask White's bishop to commit.} 8...h6 9.Bxf6 {The most common move (44%) is to capture. White cannot play Bh4 and maintain the pin, as we can trap the bishop with g6-g5.} 9...Qxf6 {Black has a solid position and the bishop pair. The queen will typically retreat to e7 or d8, and after developing the remaining pieces to natural squares, Black can consider expanding with moves like ...f5 or ... a5.} *